BRIEF COMMUNICATION
GAD65 autoimmunity after treatment with nivolumab:
a multifocal presentation
Giorgia Teresa Maniscalco1,2 & Anastasia Zekeridou3,4 & Lia Allegorico2 & Angelo Ranieri1 & Massimo Napolitano1 &
Marianna Pezzella5 & Luigi Della Gatta 6 & Valentino Manzo1 & Sergio Ferrari7 & Sara Mariotto7
Received: 6 April 2021 / Accepted: 5 May 2021
# Fondazione Società Italiana di Neurologia 2021
Abstract
Introduction Neurological disorders are considered rare complications of immune-checkpoint inhibitor.
Case description We report a 63-year-old man with recurrence of melanoma who presented epilepsy, limbic encephalitis,
cerebellar ataxia, and stiff person syndrome soon after treatment with nivolumab, an immune-checkpoint inhibitor. On autoim-
mune screening, serum and CSF GAD65 were detected. Significant response to steroids and intravenous immunoglobulins were
observed, but cancer recurred after nivolumab discontinuation in parallel with epileptic seizure and worsening of cognitive
impairment and the patient died.
Discussion This case expands the spectrum of GAD65-associated conditions induced by immune-checkpoint inhibitor and
underlines treatment complexity when both neurological complications and tumour recurrence occur.
Keywords Epilepsy . Encephalitis . Ataxia . Stiff person syndrome . Checkpoint inhibitor . GAD65
Introduction
Nivolumab is an immune-checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) targeting
programmed cell death protein-1, approved for the treatment
of non-small cell lung cancer, renal cancer, advanced
urothelial carcinoma, treatment-refractory Hodgkin lympho-
ma, and melanoma. Different immune-related adverse events
have been related to nivolumab [ 1]. In particular, CNS com-
plications are rare in relation to ICI, and overall neurological
complications appear in 1% of patients treated with
monotherapy and 2 –3% of cases under combined therapy
[2]. In this context, 0.1–0.2% of cases present limbic enceph-
alitis during ICI treatment with or without well-known neuro-
nal antibodies [3, 4].
We herein present a patient with metastatic melanoma who
developed epilepsy, limbic encephalitis, cerebellar ataxia, and
stiff person syndrome after monotherapy with nivolumab,
covering the whole spectrum of manifestations described with
GAD65 autoimmunity.
<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">Case report
A 63-year-old man presented with recurrent generalized epi-
leptic seizures for one day. Memory loss, behavioural chang-
es, and walking difficulties were also reported for the last 4
weeks. The patient had a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus
and melanoma, surgically removed 12 years ago. His melano-
ma recurred with lung involvement and was treated with
nivolumab for 3 months before neurological presentation.
On admission, neurological examination showed spatial
and temporal disorientation, mild persistent left hemiparesis,
lower extremity spasticity, hyperreflexia, and bilateral
Babinski signs, an ataxic syndrome with inability to walk,
asymmetric upper limb dysmetria, and bilateral horizontal</seg>
